  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/cd/Tales_of_Suspense_Vol_1_46.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The first four and a half pages introduce us to Professor Vanko, [SPAN: severity=error; text=Red Russia's] greatest expert on electricity, who shows off his new red suit for the nation's leader, Khrushchev.&nbsp; It has the ability to disrupt \"every electronic contraption Iron Man possesses!\"&nbsp; They want to take out Iron Man because he is always protecting Tony Stark, whom they want to kill to weaken the US defense efforts.\n\nTwo weeks later, as Stark sends up a test rocket, the Crimson Dynamo is near the launch site and disrupts the rocket, causing it to plummet toward Earth, and Iron Man is forced to rush up and save it.&nbsp; Crimson Dynamo sees that his leader was correct -- Iron Man <i>does</i> always seem to be around to protect Stark's inventions.&nbsp; Soon, he's attacking Stark's plants across the country.&nbsp; After the tenth one, Stark realizes he's being targeted, and the fortune he's made selling military equipment to the US government is being threatened.&nbsp; There's now even talk in the government that Stark is sabotaging his own companies to help the Reds!\n\nBut Crimson Dynamo, even though he's successfully ruined Stark's defense efforts (which is what the Russians <i>actually </i>wanted), <i>really</i> wants to face Iron Man to show that he's superior.&nbsp; Finally, he attacks the location where Stark himself is present, certain that Iron Man will inevitably show up.&nbsp; He does... and they finally face off.\n\nIron Man has guessed by now that he's disrupting nearby electrical impulses and sets up an \"electrical force field.\"&nbsp; Meanwhile, he secretly records Crimson Dynamo's confession that he was behind the sabotage at the various plants, to help prove Stark wasn't responsible.&nbsp; Crimson Dynamo did not bargain on being unable to disrupt Iron Man's suit, and is soon defeated, especially when Iron Man simulates an order from the Communist leader to terminate him upon his return!\n\nCrimson Dynamo is enraged that he was to be so quickly thrown under the bus and agrees to defect to the US, and uncover enough covert funds to pay for all the damage he's caused, and Stark even offers him a job, figuring that by robbing the Reds of a brilliant scientist, he more than makes up for the losses.\n\nKhrushchev is furious and vows revenge!",
  "grade_one_liner": "Language snafus aside, the sabotage setup makes for a mildly enjoyable outing, even if the Dynamo feels thin.&nbsp; A light Cold War skirmish that doesn't linger.",
  "subtitle": "Bluster in Red Armor",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=<i>Oh boy</i>, here we go again!&nbsp; Another case of mismatched languages!]\n\nIt's especially noticeable here because the plot hinges on international espionage, and the illusion of geopolitical tension is harder to maintain when everyone sounds like they're chatting in midtown Manhattan.\n\nThe story takes its time introducing Professor Vanko, the [SPAN: severity=error; text=Red Russian] scientist who has invented the new Crimson Dynamo armor so that he can go to the United States and stop Stark's operations and thereby weaken the superpower's progress in the arms race.\n\nThe armor is necessary to get past Iron Man, who is always nearby to guard Stark.\n[ASSET: center | CrimsonDynamoIntro | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=Communists wanting to sabotage the United States is a reasonable enough plan, so <b>no points off just for that</b>.]\n\nBut the over three-and-a-half-page conversation and demonstration Vanko has with Russian leader Khrushchev (though the leader's name is never given -- it is <i>obvious </i>enough who he is) is presented entirely in English, and we as readers can only assume this is for our convenience, so that we can understand what's going on.\n\nIt's a surprisingly long runway for a villain introduction, almost <i>indulgent</i> compared to the brisk pacing Marvel has been leaning toward in its current books.\n\n<b>Again...this is not a problem</b>, but we <i>know</i> Russian is <i>actually</i> being spoken because the third panel on page 5 has dialogue that begins with \"Da!\"&nbsp; (Earlier, on page 3, Vanko says \"Yes...\")\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Park that bit of knowledge aside for now -- I'll come back to it...]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nPages 6-8 are spent showing Stark's facilities being sabotaged by the new Crimson Dynamo, and he does such a good job that Stark realizes he's become a target for enemy agents, while at the same time, the US government begins to wonder if Stark is doing this deliberately.&nbsp; Is he actually a Communist <i>himself</i>?\n\nIt's one of the few times the title leans fully into Cold War paranoia, letting the reader feel how fragile Stark's reputation is beneath all that polished confidence.\n[ASSET: center | IsStarkACommunist | width=67%]\n\n<b>Once again</b>, all this is reasonably well told, and gets the reader to the bottom of page 8, with only five pages left to go.&nbsp; At no point did I feel inclined to put the book down.&nbsp; Admittedly, it wasn't any <i>thriller</i>, but the thought of Stark himself being suspected as a Communist sympathizer was both logically consistent -- not to mention intriguing.\n\nIt's also one of the rare moments when Stark's public persona becomes a <i>liability</i> rather than an <i>asset</i>, which gives the middle of the issue more texture than the usual sabotage‑and‑counter‑sabotage routine.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | CantAffectIronMan | width=32%] Vanko should have stopped there because his actual mission was accomplished, but <i>of course</i>, he was too vain for that and wanted an actual face-off against Iron Man.\n[BR]\n[BR] It's a pretty standard pivot:&nbsp; the mission is accomplished, but the ego isn't satisfied (so the <i>Super-Villain Handbook</i> suggests there's more to do).&nbsp; And so he crashes into a building where he suspects Stark of being and counting on Iron Man showing up to protect him.\n[BR]\n[BR] Vanko is <i>correct</i>, but within seconds, he finds Iron Man a little harder to take down than he bargained on.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Were you surprised by this?)]\n\n[ASSET: right | CaughtConfessing | width=28%] Not even one page later, Iron Man already has Professor Vanko's confession on tape, complete with proof that Stark didn't sabotage his own plants.\n[BR]\n[BR] And... let's just say that the \"ultra-sensitive\" miniature reel-to-reel tape recorder inside of Iron Man's armor that we see at the top of page 11 is <b><i>classic</i></b>, causing me to laugh out loud.\n[BR]\n[BR] <i>Easily</i> a finalist for this month's Vintage Moment Award -- <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n[BR] It's also a perfect snapshot of early‑'60s futurism, where every problem can be solved by shrinking a gadget until it fits inside a metal glove.\n\nIron Man makes short work of Crimson Dynamo after that, surrounding him with the wood from nearby trees.&nbsp; He then excuses himself from the battle, only to return \"minutes later\" and inform the Russian that he's picked up a \"radio message from behind the Iron Curtain\" on his built-in receiver.\n\nThe message indicates that Khrushchev is planning to eliminate him as soon as he returns!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold-italic; text=GASP!]\n\nIt's a convenient twist, but it does give the story a clean way to flip Vanko's allegiance without resorting to a prolonged moral crisis.\n\nWhat is Vanko to do?&nbsp; <i>Heh</i>, <b>thank Iron Man</b> for saving his life!\n[ASSET: center | RecruitTheEnemy | width=67%]\n\nAbrupt?&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>, but it's also in keeping with Marvel's evolving habit of resolving international conflicts with a single well‑timed revelation.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And let's not forget that he <i>defects to the US</i>... and accepts an offer to work at Stark Industries!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=Khrushchev is furious!]\n\nBut the US now has stolen a top \"research man\" from the [SPAN: severity=error; text=Reds], so once again, the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=<i>So... <b>what's the problem?</b></i>]\n\nIf you haven't guessed by now, just like four months ago, back in [LINK:1963:TOS_42_iron0|TOS #42], [SPAN: severity=error; text=Stark can't possibly be expected to know Russian] in order to record a supposedly-intercepted message broadcast from the Iron Curtain (as if those just happen to be floating about the airwaves in the <i>first </i>place)!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I might also point out that Professor Vanko <i>clearly</i> must be able to speak English so that he can talk to Iron Man.]\n\nI took major points off for this the first time, and the story got a low grade (again, see [LINK:1963:TOS_42_iron0|TOS #42]), but I'm now beginning to see that this is just part of the storytelling genre of the era.&nbsp; The Thing has a similar exchange with an ancient Egyptian on page 18 of this month's [LINK:1963:FF_19|FF #19], and I didn't call it out there either, so I am forced to now conclude that it's best not to overthink it.\n\nMarvel clearly isn't interested in linguistic realism -- clarity for the reader trumps authenticity every time.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; text=But <i>still</i>... can't Vanko tell he's hearing <b>English</b> words instead of Russian?&nbsp; (Is this not too much to ask?)]\n\nWhile the Crimson Dynamo turns out to be mostly bluster with little to back up his words (you have to admit he wasn't much of a match for Iron Man), the story still held up reasonably well (outside of its one major [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"What were you <i>thinking</i>?\"] moment).&nbsp; I might even go so far as to call it pleasant, here and there.\n\nI didn't mind at <i>all</i> that we didn't see Iron Man until page 6, and the panel of the reel-to-reel tape recorder made me [SPAN: severity=success; text=laugh-out-loud] (though it's not allowed to add points for simply being a product of its day).\n\nHappy and Pepper aren't given much to do here.&nbsp; Since they'd just been introduced last month, it was almost a [SPAN: severity=info; text=contractual obligation] on the part of the creators that they be featured again, though I question why Happy is simply sitting around Stark's office instead of out at the garage polishing something.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But I'm no wealthy industrialist and am hardly in the position to say what chauffeurs should do when they're not driving.)]\n\nThe somewhat cheesy image of Khrushchev ranting and raving (and throwing things) in the final panel -- with the Kremlin visible in the background -- was the [SPAN: severity=success; text=added bit of hilarity] that elevated the story above mere reader level.\n\nIt's not one for the history books, but it provided readers with a reasonable-enough distraction -- part Cold War thriller, part gadget showcase, part morality play --exactly the blend this feature seems to be settling into.\"\n\n(<i>Speaking</i> of readers, I'm sure I'm not the only one wondering:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=what will become of the Crimson Dynamo?])",
  "side_note": [
    "The back-up tale about the poor, down-on-his-luck reporter \"Fenwick Flubb\" was somewhat amusing, with art by Sol Brodsky, which made it look more suitable as a cartoony-type tale, which indeed it mostly was, but of course, with the standard twist ending you've come to expect from magazines like this!"
  ],
  "ownership": "As for my own copy, a lower-grade one came to me as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid $16.50, and it sold on eBay in June, 2021 for $99, netting me almost $70.",
  "highlight": "Iron Man's brush with the new Crimson Dynamo offers some Cold War color, even if the payoff barely registers."
}